This is the payoff of keeping `Appender` an interface: a brand-new destination is a brand-new class, registered at startup, with zero edits to `Logger`.

```python
class Appender(ABC):
    @abstractmethod
    def write(self, formatted: str) -> None: ...

class CloudAppender(Appender):
    def __init__(self, client):
        self.client = client

    def write(self, formatted: str) -> None:
        self.client.send_log(formatted)

logger.add_appender(CloudAppender(cloud_client))  # wiring only, no Logger edit
```

`Logger.log()` only ever calls `appender.write(formatted)` in a loop over whatever's registered — it has no idea `CloudAppender` exists. This is the open/closed principle in practice: open for a new appender, closed to modifying `Logger`.

The caller-facing API doesn't change — it's the internals that grow a queue and a dedicated consumer.

```python
class AsyncLogger:
    def __init__(self, appenders, queue_size=10_000):
        self.queue = queue.Queue(maxsize=queue_size)
        self.appenders = appenders
        threading.Thread(target=self._drain, daemon=True).start()

    def log(self, record: LogRecord) -> None:
        self.queue.put_nowait(record)   # returns immediately

    def _drain(self) -> None:
        while True:
            record = self.queue.get()   # blocks only the writer thread
            formatted = self.formatter.format(record)
            for appender in self.appenders:
                appender.write(formatted)
```

`log()` is now O(1) enqueue work on the caller's thread; all the slow I/O happens on `_drain`'s own thread, decoupled from request latency.
